# High-Performance As-Cast Nonfullerene Polymer Solar Cells with Thicker Active Layer and Large Area Exceeding 11% Power Conversion Efficiency

## abstract
In this work, a nonfullerene polymer solar cell (PSC) based on a wide bandgap polymer donor PM6 containing fluorinated thienyl benzodithiophene (BDT-2F) unit and a narrow bandgap small molecule acceptor 2,2-((2Z,2Z)-((4,4,9,9-tetrahexyl-4,9-dihydro-s-indaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b]dithiophene-2,7-diyl)bis(methanylylidene))bis(3-oxo-2,3-dihydro-1H-indene-2,1-diylidene))dimalononitrile (IDIC) is developed. In addition to matched energy levels and complementary absorption spectrum with IDIC, PM6 possesses high crystallinity and strong - stacking alignment, which are favorable to charge carrier transport and hence suppress recombination in devices. As a result, the PM6:IDIC-based PSCs without extra treatments show an outstanding power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 11.9%, which is the record value for the as-cast PSC devices reported in the literature to date. Moreover, the device performances are insensitive to the active layer thickness (95-255 nm) and device area (0.20-0.81 cm2 ) with PCEs of over 11%. Besides, the PM6:IDIC-based flexible PSCs with a large device area of 1.25 cm2 exhibit a high PCE of 6.54%. These results indicate that the PM6:IDIC blend is a promising candidate for future roll-to-roll mass manufacturing and practical application of highly efficient PSCs.

In this work, a nonfullerene polymer solar cell (PSC) based on a wide bandgap polymer donor PM6 containing fluorinated thienyl benzodithiophene (BDT-2F) unit and a narrow bandgap small molecule acceptor $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -( $(22,2^{\prime}2)$ - ((4,4,9,9-tetrahexyl-4,9-dihydro-s-indaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b′]dithiophene-2,7-diyl) bis(methanylylidene))bis(3-oxo-2,3-dihydro-1H-indene-2,1-diylidene))dimalononitrile (IDIC) is developed. In addition to matched energy levels and complementary absorption spectrum with IDIC, PM6 possesses high crystallinity and strong $\pmb{\pi}\mathbf{-}\pmb{\pi}$ stacking alignment, which are favorable to charge carrier transport and hence suppress recombination in devices. As a result, the PM6:IDICbased PSCs without extra treatments show an outstanding power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $11.9\%$ , which is the record value for the as-cast PSC devices reported in the literature to date. Moreover, the device performances are insensitive to the active layer thickness $(\approx95-255~\mathrm{{nm}})$ ) and device area $(0.20-0.81~\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ with PCEs of over $11\%$ . Besides, the PM6:IDIC-based flexible PSCs with a large device area of $1.25c m^{2}$ exhibit a high PCE of $6.54\%$ . These results indicate that the PM6:IDIC blend is a promising candidate for future roll-to-roll mass manufacturing and practical application of highly efficient PSCs.
Nonfullerene (NF)-polymer solar cells (PSCs) have attracted great attention and achieved great success, $[1-6]$ and the power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) have reached $10\mathrm{-}13\%$ in the past few years.[7–12]
Compared to the fullerene acceptors, the n-type organic semiconductor (n-OS) acceptors possess distinctively advantages, such as highly adjustable optical absorption, readily tunable frontier energy levels, low production cost, and large material selections.[13–20] Among the n-OS acceptors, the narrow bandgap n-OS materials with acceptor– donor–acceptor (A–D–A) structure, such as $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -[[6,6,12,12-tetrakis(4-hexylphenyl)- 6,12-­dihydrodithieno $[2,3\mathrm{-d};2^{\prime},3^{\prime}\mathrm{-d}^{\prime}]$ -sindaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b′]dithiophene-2,8-diyl] bis[methylidyne(3-oxo-1H-indene-2,1(3H)- diylidene)]]bispropanedinitrile (ITIC)[18] and IDIC,[19] have become the dominated acceptor for the high performance PSCs. When the n-OS acceptors are mixed with the middle bandgap donor polymer, the blend films can obtain wide and complementary absorption spectra of $\approx350{\mathrm{-}}800\ \mathrm{\nm}$ resulting in high short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ and PCE values.
Although the success of NF-PSCs, the device fabrication can be quite tricky, which requires systematic optimization by using thermal annealing, solvent vapor annealing, or specific amount of solvent additives.[7–19] Without extra treatments, the efficiency of the NF-PSCs can only get to a level in-between $9\mathrm{-}10\%$ , as shown in Figure S1 and Table S1 (Supporting Information).[21–29] The complexity of the active layer fabrication procedure, and the complicated morphology formation kinetics, makes the NF-PSC optimization difficult. It would be of a more serious issue when large scaled roll-to-roll fabrication is implemented. Thus a simple material system, plus an easy and stable fabrication procedure, is in high demand.
The matching of donor polymer with n-OS acceptor is quite important in achieving high PCE values of the PSCs. And the quality of donor polymer largely dictates film fabrication. Thus fine-tuning donor polymer structure to enable better electronic properties and processing capability is the key to further promote photovoltaic performance of the NF-PSCs. A readily accessible methodology is to introduce fluorine substitutes on the polymer backbone,[30] which can efficiently reduce the frontier energy levels, enhance material extinction coefficient, as well as tune the morphology. The large electron negative fluorine atoms can induce multiple intramolecular interactions, which usually result in better backbone planarity and crystallinity, improving mobility and blend morphology.[31] We found that the NF-bulk heterojunction (BHJ) blend morphology can be well modified when fluorine atoms were introduced to the conjugated thienyl substituents of benzodithiophene (BDT-2F)- based polymers, and the NF-PSCs achieved a $10.4\%$ efficiency without extra treatments.[20] But, the photovoltaic performance of the NF-PSCs was quite sensitive to the active layer thickness, and the PCE was reduced to $8.9\%$ with an active layer of more than $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Developing a high thickness tolerated NF-PSC can thus be quite important, especially when roll-to-roll production is on the agenda.
Our previous work has reported a high performance polymer donor PM6 based on BDT-2F unit (Figure 1a),[30a] which shows a large bandgap of $1.80~\mathrm{eV},$ a deep the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) level of $-5.50\ \mathrm{eV},$ strong crystallinity, and a dominant face on packing with a small $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of $3.78\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Consequently, the PM6:[6,6]-phenyl- $\mathrm{\cdotC}_{71}$ -butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM})$ -based PSCs achieve a high PCE of $9.2\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of up to 0.98 V. These characteristics indicate that PM6 could be a promising wide bandgap polymer for the NFPSCs based on narrow bandgap $\mathrm{\n-OS}$ acceptor. Herein, we fabricated the NF-PSCs based on PM6 as donor and an n-OS IDIC as acceptor. The PM6:IDIC blend films show the complementary absorption spectrum from 300 to $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1b), which facilitates obtaining of high $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ in PSCs. Notably, in chlorobenzene solution of PM6, the absorption intensity of shoulder peak gradually decreases with increasing temperature from 20 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which means that PM6 can form the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction in low temperature solution. Moreover, the absorption spectrum of PM6 solution under $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ still shows an obvious shoulder peak, suggesting that PM6 can form the stable intermolecular aggregation in high temperature solution (Figure S2, Supporting Information). For the molecular energy level alignments, the PM6:IDIC pair shows a small $\Delta E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ of $0.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 1c), which facilitates achieving of a higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and lower the energy loss during the exciton dissociation in PSCs.[25] Furthermore, the PM6:IDIC blend exhibits a $\Delta E_{\mathrm{LUMO}}$ of $0.30~\mathrm{eV},$ providing sufficient driving force for charge separation. Moreover, the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction in solution, high crystallinity, small $d$ -spacing, and small $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance in film of PM6 are beneficial to forming purer polymer domain, good phase separation morphology, and suppressing geminate recombination for the active layer, and ultimately, the related PSCs with a thick active layer can still achieve a high efficiency.[32] As a result, the PSCs based on the as-cast PM6:IDIC blend achieve a PCE of up to $11.9\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.97 V, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $17.8\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and fill factor (FF) of $69\%$ . To our knowledge, the PCE of $11.9\%$ is the highest value reported in the literatures to date for the PSCs without extra treatments (see Figure S1 in the Supporting Information).[21–29] Notably, the device performances are insensitive to the film thickness of active layer from ${\approx}95$ to $255\ \mathrm{nm}$ and device area from 0.20 to $0.81\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ with PCEs of over $11\%$ . Moreover, the PM6:IDIC-based flexible PSCs also show an excellent PCE of $6.54\%$ with a device area of $1.25~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The results imply that the PM6:IDIC blend is a promising candidate for the future large scale roll-to-roll fabrication of high-performance PSCs.
The NF-PSCs with a device structure of indium tin oxide (ITO)/poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrene-sulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS)/PM6:IDIC/poly[(9,9-bis( $3^{\prime}$ - ( N , N - d i m e t - hyl)-nethylammoinium-propyl)-2,7-fluorene)-alt-2,7-(9,9- dioctylfluorene)]dibromide (PFN-Br)/Al were fabricated. As shown in Figure S3a and Table S2 (Supporting Information), the D/A weight ratios have an obvious effect on the photovoltaic performance of the PSCs. The PSCs exhibit an almost constant $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.96–0.97 V, a fluctuating $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $15.9{-}17.8\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $64-69\%$ , where the optimal PSC with a $\mathrm{{D/A}}$ ratio of 1:1 shows a champion PCE of $11.9\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.97 V, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $17.8\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $69\%$ . Notably, the PCE of $11.9\%$ is the highest value reported in the literatures to date for the PSCs without extra treatments. Figure S3b (Supporting Information) shows the corresponding external quantum efficiency (EQE) plots. The PSC based on PM6:IDIC (1:1, w/w) displayed a wide EQE response in the wavelength region of $300{\mathrm{-}}790~\mathrm{nm}$ with a maximum value of $81.1\%$ , indicating efficient photoelectron conversion in the device. Moreover, the high EQE values in the absorption region of IDIC $(650{-}790\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) indicate that the photons absorbed by IDIC are efficiently converted into electrons despite the relatively small $\Delta E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ of $0.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ between PM6 and IDIC (Figure 1c), which is in concordance with the high photoluminescence (PL) quenching efficiency (Figure S4, Supporting Information). In addition, the integral $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values agree quite well with the measured $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values and the differences between them are below $5\%$ .
In the following text, we further probed the relationship between the device performance and different processing conditions such as the active layer thickness and area, thermal annealing, and solvent additive treatments. Figure 2a and Table 1 display the $J{-}V$ curves and device parameters of the PSCs with difference active layer thicknesses, respectively. It is obvious that the device efficiencies were insensitive to the variation of the active layer thicknesses from ${\approx}95$ to $255\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2c). The champion PCE of $11.9\%$ was achieved for the device with an active layer thickness of $150\mathrm{nm}$ . Notably, regardless of the thin film $(\approx95\mathrm{nm})$ or thick film $(\approx255\:\mathrm{nm})$ for the active layer, the PCEs of devices are still over $11\%$ due to the almost unchanged $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ $(\approx0.97\pm0.01$ V) and FF $(\approx68\pm1\%)$ , and the slightly fluctuating $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ $(\approx17.2\pm0.6\:\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ values. Moreover, the average PCE of the PSCs reached up to $11.5\pm0.4\%$ in the active layer thickness from ${\approx}95$ to $255\mathrm{nm}$ .
Figure 2d and Figure S5 (Supporting Information) show the $J{-}V$ and EQE plots of the PSCs with an active layer thickness of $150~\mathrm{nm}$ under different device areas of 0.20, 0.45, and $0.81~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, and the device parameters were listed in Table 1. With the increase of active area from 0.20 to $0.81~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , the PSCs show slightly decreased $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ from 0.97 to $0.95\mathrm{V}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ from 17.8 to $16.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , while the FF is slightly increased from $69\%$ to $71\%$ . Finally, the PSCs with a larger device area of $0.81~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ still achieved an encouraging PCE of $11.4\%$ . Notably, the PCEs of the PM6:IDIC-based PSCs with different active layer thicknesses are also insensitive to the device area. As shown in Figure S6 and Table S3 (Supporting Information), with the device area increasing from 0.2 to $0.81~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , the devices based on thinner films $(95~\mathrm{nm})$ ) or thicker films $(255~\mathrm{nm})$ also show PCEs of over $10\%$ . The flexible devices based on PM6:IDIC with the structure of polyethylene terephthalate (PET)/Ag-grid/PH1000/PEDOT:PSS/PM6:IDIC/ nano-particles (NP)-ZnO/Ag were also fabricated (as shown in Figure S7 in the Supporting Information).[33] The flexible devices with an active layer area of $1.25~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ show a PCE of $6.54\%$ with initial optimization. The above results indicate that the PM6:IDIC blend is a promising candidate for the future large scale roll-toroll fabrication of high performance PSCs.
As shown in Figure S3c (Supporting Information), the PSC annealed at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ shows a lower PCE of $10.5\%$ in comparison with the as-cast PSCs. Moreover, the PSC treated with $1\%$ (v/v) 1,8-diiodooctane (DIO) also displays a decreased PCE of $10.7\%$ (Figure S3c, Supporting Information). The above results imply that the extra treatments are unnecessary and the PM6:IDIC blend has a quite stable morphology upon different treatment conditions, which facilitates the large-area manufacturing of the PSCs in the future.
To probe the charge generation and dissociation process of the PM6:IDIC-based PSCs with different processing conditions, plots of the photocurrent $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ versus effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ of the PSCs were measured (Figure 2e). The parameter definitions and calculation methods are described in detail in the Supporting Information.[34] The exciton dissociation probability $(P(E,\ T))$ is determined from the ratio of $J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}.$ Under the maximal power output conditions, $P(E,\ T)$ values were calculated as $90.5\%$ , $88.6\%$ , and $86.4\%$ for the devices based on PM6:IDIC blends ascast and with thermal annealing or $1\%$ DIO, respectively. The higher $P(E,T)$ indicates that the device without extra treatments has higher exciton dissociation and charge extraction efficiencies, which is in agreement with the high PCE of the related PSCs.
More insight into the charge recombination process in the devices, the dependence of $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ under different light intensities $(P)$ was performed (Figure 2f). The relationship between $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ and $P$ is defined as $J_{\mathrm{ph}}\propto P\boldsymbol{\mathrm S}$ . Weak bimolecular recombination in the device would result in a linear dependence of $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ on $P$ with S value close to $1.^{[34]}$ The S values of the fitted lines in logarithmic coordinates for the PSCs as-cast and with thermal annealing or $1\%$ DIO are 0.989, 0.985, and 0.980, respectively. The higher S value for the PSC without extra treatments means less bimolecular recombination in PSCs.
PL spectra of the PM6 and IDIC pure films and their blends with different processing conditions were measured (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The PM6:IDIC blends without extra treatments showed more than $94\%$ PL quenching compared to the PL spectra of the pure PM6 or IDIC, which is higher than those of the blends with thermal annealing or $1\%$ DIO treatment. High PL quenching efficiency means that the PM6:IDIC blends without extra treatments have an effective photoinduced exciton dissociation and charge transport, which is in agreement with the higher EQE and PCE values of the related devices.
Thin film structure order was evaluated by grazing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXD).[20–23] Figure 3a,b shows the GIXD 2D profiles and the related out-of-plane (OOP) and in-plane (IP) line-cut profiles. It is quite distinctive that all BHJ films had a $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking peak with OOP preference, and a mixed diffraction rings coming from PM6 and IDIC. In linecut profiles, two closely adjacent peaks at 0.31 and $0.41\ \mathrm{~A~}^{-1}$ were seen, which came from PM6 and IDIC, respectively. The crystal coherence length (CCL) was estimated to be 9.0 and $7.7\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , respectively. Thus, both components readily order upon simple spin cast. The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking peak is located at $1.78\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ in OOP direction, which composes of structure feature of both materials and cannot be separated. Thermal annealing does not change the crystalline order much for both materials except for a sharp OOP direction peak at $0.73{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ that is hard to directly allocate the origin. $1\%$ of DIO additive strongly induces the molecular packing of IDIC. As seen from IP diffraction profile, the PM6 and IDIC (100) peaks became sharper and better resolved. The CCL of PM6 is estimated to be $9.0\mathrm{nm}$ that is similar to that of the as-cast film; the CCL of IDIC is estimated to be $22.5~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is significantly larger than that in the as-cast film. Meanwhile, the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking becomes much sharper and centered at ${\approx}1.82~\mathrm{A^{-1}}$ , with more contributions from IDIC. Thus, the DIO treatment can significantly enhance the crystallization of IDIC acceptor. Smaller molecular sized IDIC can thus benefit from a faster crystallization kinetics to form large crystallites to sharpen the morphology of BHJ film. This effect can be supported by the azimuthal distribution of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking peak of IDIC, which becomes more intensified in IP direction, due to the fact of free crystallization of mole­ cules that can grow in all directions.
The phase separation of BHJ films processed under different conditions is probed by resonant soft X-ray scattering (RSoXS) using $284.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ photon energy (Figure 3c).[35] The as-cast film exhibited a broad distribution of phase separation from 0.003 to $0.017{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ with a hump centered at $0.0084\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ . Thus, the average center to center distance is ${\approx}75~\mathrm{nm}$ , which means a statistics average domain size of ${\approx}37.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Thermal annealing though does not change much the crystallization of the materials in BHJ film, the phase separation becomes more intensive and shifted to a lower $q$ region with a scattering hump centered at $0.0047\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ , a center to center distance of ${\approx}134~\mathrm{nm}$ . DIO additive is even more efficient in driving the phase separation, as seen from strongly elevated RSoXS signals. A scattering hump located at $0.0045{\mathrm{~A}}^{-1}$ with a distance of ${\approx}140~\mathrm{nm}$ is seen. Moreover, the exciton diffusion length of PM6 determined by time-resolved photoluminescence (Figure S8, Supporting Information) is $16\pm6$ nm, which is in good agreement with the domain size radius of ${\approx}18.75~\mathrm{nm}$ for the as-cast film, which indicates that the excitons generated in PM6 can diffuse well to the D/A interface (the calculated exciton dissociation efficiency reaches ${\approx}92.1\%$ for the as-cast film vs $\approx78.5\%$ for the blend films with thermal annealing or DIO treatments). Hence, an excessive phase separation size will impede exciton effective separation, charge transport, and collection, resulting in lower PCE values in PSCs,[30a,34] which agrees with the photovoltaic performance of the related devices.
The surface and bulk morphologies of BHJ films were probed using atomic force microscopy (AFM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Quite flat surface AFM and TEM images were seen for the blends as-cast (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Thus, the phase separation is weak in this case, which agrees well with scattering investigation (see Figure 3). Thermal annealed blends showed corrugated surface texture, reflecting internal structure change. In the corresponding TEM image, large scaled density fluctuation was shown, which corresponded to the RSoXS length scales. Thus, thermal annealing changes the morphology by inducing material aggregation, not by crystallization. DIO processing changes morphology quite different from thermal annealing. As seen from AFM and TEM, small sized flakes with size of a few tens of nanometers were displayed. This feature corresponds to the IP (100) crystal sizes, which is usually larger than the estimated CCL. These features are good for hole transport and the strong RSoXS intensity indicates a good phase separation quality. Thus, though length scale is shifted to a large value, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of device remains large. The problem of FF reduction can be issues related to interfaces that reduced the overall device performances. The blend film ascast possesses a smaller $R_{\mathfrak{q}}$ of $1.69\mathrm{nm}$ compared to those blend films with thermal annealing $(2.16\mathrm{nm})$ and $1\%$ DIO $(5.46\mathrm{nm})$ , respectively. The over-roughened surface of the active layer will impede the charge extraction at the interfaces of the devices, eventually leading to lower FF and PCE values in PSCs.
Figure 4a summarizes the correlation of $e V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ against optical bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}}^{\mathrm{{\opt}}})$ in this work in comparison with the other efficient $(\mathrm{PCE}>8\%$ ) PSCs based on polymer donor and n-OS acceptor (see Table S4 in the Supporting Information). In most cases, the main PCE decrease in PSCs is due to the loss of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . It is obvious that the majority of the NF-PSCs show $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ $(E_{\mathrm{loss}}=E_{\mathrm{g}}^{\ \mathrm{opt}}-e V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of over the empirical threshold of $0.6~\mathrm{eV}.$ Only a few $\mathrm{D/A}$ blends with matched energy levels have been exhibited to maximize the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and minimize the $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ values. Here, we reported a $\mathrm{{\DeltaD/A}}$ pair of PM6:IDIC that can obtain high photovoltage at small voltage loss. Moreover, the PSC displays a small $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ of $0.59\mathrm{eV}$ due to the small $E_{\mathrm{g}}^{\mathrm{\opt}}$ $(1.56~\mathrm{eV})$ of the IDIC film and the high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (0.97 V) of the optimal device, which is much lower than that of the other polymer:IDIC blends.[19,24,29] However, the PSCs are usually difficult to obtain high EQE value at small $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ due to the trade-off between $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . Recent results indicated that higher EQE values over $70\%$ can be obtained at $E_{\mathrm{loss}}$ less than $0.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ in PSCs by rationally matching $\mathrm{{\DeltaD/A}}$ pair.[4a,8,11,15] Here, the $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{loss}}}$ is even smaller than the empirical limit of $0.6\mathrm{eV},$ , the PSC still achieves $\mathrm{EQE}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of up to $81.1\%$ and high PCE of $11.9\%$ (Figure 4b,c), which can be attributed to the very small $\Delta E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}$ of $0.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ between PM6 and IDIC.[25]
In conclusion, an efficient PSC based on a polymer donor PM6 containing BDT-2F unit and an n-OS acceptor IDIC was developed. In addition to energy levels matching and absorption complementary to IDIC, polymer PM6 also possesses strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction and intermolecular aggregation effect in solution, high crystallinity, small $d$ -spacing, and small $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance in film, which are beneficial to obtaining high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ forming good phase separation morphology and suppressing geminate recombination in PSCs. As a result, the PM6:IDIC-based PSCs without extra treatments displayed a high PCE of $11.9\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.97 V, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $17.8~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $69\%$ . Notably, the PCE value of $11.9\%$ is the highest value reported in the literatures to date for the PSCs without extra treatments. Moreover, the device performances are insensitive to the active layer thickness $(\approx95-255~\mathrm{nm}$ ) and device area $(0.20{-}0.81\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ with PCEs of over $11\%$ . These results imply that the PM6:IDIC blend is a promising candidate for the large scale roll-to-roll fabrication of high-performance PSCs.
